# Addon Renderers

- Before a **new addon GUI** or a **large new control surface** (new
  `component_type`, new page, or many new interactive elements), run the
  research gate in `framework-gui-appearance.mdc`: pick an archetype from
  `framework-gui-page-patterns.mdc`, read appearance / icon-button guidance,
  open peer sections that already solve a similar job, and prefer system
  primitives over a bespoke panel. A thin stub in the target addon does not
  skip that pass.
- Add GUI integration through the addon's root `index.js`. Export a named `renderers` object and keep renderer implementations under the addon's `gui/` code.
- Map each renderer key exactly to the backend payload's stable `component_type`. The value must be the React component responsible for that JSON contract.
- Do not edit or commit `framework/runtimes/gui/src/addonGui.generated.js`. It is an untracked build artifact regenerated from the mounted addon root at dev-server start and whenever that root changes; tracking it lets one deployment's `FRAMEWORK_INFRASTRUCTURE__CUSTOM_ADDONS` selection leak into another's.
- Resolve `@addons/*` through the `frameworkAddonsResolver` Vite plugin only. Do not add a `resolve.alias` for that prefix — Vite runs alias resolution ahead of user `pre` plugins, which silently shadows the plugin's multi-root search and its missing-entrypoint fallback. The plugin must return `realpath`s so a module reached through the symlink farm and the same module reached relatively keep one id.
- A listed-but-absent addon entrypoint must degrade, never break the boot. Vite fails the whole module graph on an unresolvable specifier even when the import is dynamic, so the resolver stubs a missing `@addons/<name>/index.js` with an empty module and the GUI bootstrap skips addon modules that fail to load. Deep imports and bare core specifiers still fail loudly.
- A renderer consumes serialized data, typically as a `component` prop. It must not depend on Python classes, query the database, or reproduce backend authorization and domain decisions.
- Keep payloads JSON-safe and renderer-friendly. Coordinate backend and frontend changes whenever a required field, nested shape, or `component_type` changes.
- Reuse system renderers and layout primitives for existing component types. Add a custom renderer only when the addon introduces a genuinely new presentation contract or an intentional registered override.
- Auth registers `main_nav: AuthNavMain` and overrides system `NavMain` at runtime. Mobile or chrome changes to top navigation must update `framework/addons/auth/gui/components/AuthNavMain.jsx` (keep system `NavMain` in sync). When both desktop and mobile primary trees mount, give mobile `NavItem` nodes a distinct `idPrefix` so component names stay unique in the DOM.
- Keep custom `component_type` keys addon-specific and avoid cross-addon renderer collisions. Override an existing renderer only through an explicit, documented integration contract.
- Resolve nested payloads through the established renderer map/context. Do not add growing `component_type` conditionals or hard-coded addon imports to the GUI runtime.
- Handle missing and optional payload data defensively with explicit defaults, while surfacing invalid required contracts instead of silently inventing incompatible values.
- Preserve backend-provided stable identities for React keys and use them for DOM IDs only when they are valid and meaningful. Keep local browser-only state inside the renderer or a focused hook.
- Keep browser interaction, loading, and presentation in the renderer; keep durable state, permissions, validation, and payload composition in the API.
- Export optional addon-wide GUI wiring only when the GUI shell defines that integration contract; currently supported root exports include `renderers` and auth wiring.
- Declare addon browser dependencies in an addon `package.json`; do not add addon-only packages to the generic GUI runtime.
- Treat a missing renderer as a registration, payload, or deployment problem. Degrade at the component boundary rather than crashing the page or coupling the GUI to backend implementation.
- Add focused tests for custom renderer registration and any payload behavior the addon introduces.
- Model/CRUD record tables (`TableSection`, form `TableInput`, and equivalent custom list tables) must make the **entire row** clickable when a detail page exists. Do not rely on a single name/title cell link alone, and do not add a separate Open/Edit action badge as the drill-in path. Honor row `route` (preferred; `href`/`url` only as legacy fallbacks) with full-row click plus an accessible first-column link; ignore clicks that originate on nested interactive controls so soft-nav and action badges own those activations. Skip row navigation only when there is no detail page. Keep actions-column badges for delete and other secondary ops.
